tel 1.0

base ambience
import soundness

info   a program's surroundings — environment variables, system properties, directories, the platform — as typed contextual values
detail
    _Ambience_ represents the things a program inherits from wherever it was started — its
    **environment variables**, the JVM's **system properties**, the **working directory**, a
    **temporary directory**, the **terminal**'s capabilities, the **operating system** and
    **processor** it runs on — as typed, contextual values, in place of the raw platform's
    nullable strings reached through global static calls.

    Each source is a small trait: an `Environment` answers a variable's name with its optional
    text, a `System` answers a property's name, a `WorkingDirectory` and a `TemporaryDirectory`
    each name one directory. A method which reads from one takes it as a `using` parameter, so
    its dependence on its surroundings is visible in its signature, and a test, a sandbox or a
    daemon serving many shells can supply a different one. Reading is typed: a variable is read
    as a member of `Environment` (`Environment.editor[Text]`, where the camel-case name becomes
    `EDITOR`), a property as a dotted member path of `System.properties`
    (`System.properties.user.home[Text]()`), and each decodes to the type asked for, raising
    `Environment.Error` or `Property.Error` when the value is not set.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import systems.javaBaseSystem

    val editor: Text = Environment.editor[Text]
    val columns: Int = Environment.columns[Int]
    val separator: Char = System.properties.file.separator[Char]()
    val platform: (Os, Architecture) = (System.os, System.architecture)
    ```

    The real environment and properties are never in scope by themselves: each is a given in
    a choice package, imported by name, because `import soundness.*` does not import givens.
    The packages are `environments` (`javaBaseEnvironment`, `emptyEnvironment`, and the daemon
    and WASI ones other libraries add), `systems` (`javaBaseSystem`, `emptySystem`),
    `workingDirectories`, `temporaryDirectories`, `termcaps` and `wasiApis`. An `Environment`
    is a single function from a name to an `Optional[Text]`, so one built for a block is a
    lambda, and `variables(name = value)` lays one variable over the environment in scope.

    On top of the raw sources, `Xdg` and `Directories` give the standard directories of the
    XDG base directory specification (and their Windows equivalents) as paths of any type, a
    `Configurator` reads an application's settings from properties and variables under one
    prefix, and `Searchpath` is the marker for a path plane which searches a list of
    directories, such as the XDG data and config directories.

    The library has two source sets. `ambience.core` has everything above and compiles for
    the JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native, where the `javaBase…` givens read `java.lang.System`.
    `ambience.wasi` adds `environments.wasiEnvironment` and
    `workingDirectories.wasiWorkingDirectory`, which read the `wasi:cli/environment`
    interface of a Wasm component. Other libraries add to the choice packages: `ethereal`
    ships `environments.daemonEnvironment` and `workingDirectories.daemonClientWorkingDirectory`,
    and `exoskeleton` ships `environments.daemonClientEnvironment`.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, which has one consequence to know:
    `soundness.System` is this library's `System`, and shadows `java.lang.System` in any file
    which imports `soundness.*`. A bare `System.nanoTime`, `System.out` or `System.arraycopy`
    in such a file fails with _value nanoTime is not a member of object ambience.System_; write
    `java.lang.System.nanoTime`, or `import java.lang as jl` and `jl.System.nanoTime`.

refer ambience.Environment
refer ambience.System
refer ambience.Property
refer ambience.WorkingDirectory
refer ambience.Xdg
refer ambience.environments
refer ambience.systems

entry .Environment   the environment variables, as a contextual value read by typed member access
  detail
      An `Environment` is the source of a program's environment variables: `#variable` answers
      a name with its text, or `Unset` if it is not defined, and `#entries` enumerates every
      variable where the environment can. The real one is `environments.javaBaseEnvironment`,
      imported by name; `environments.emptyEnvironment` defines nothing; and since the trait
      has a single abstract method, a lambda from name to `Optional[Text]` is an environment
      for a test or a block.

      The companion is the way to read a variable. `Environment.editor[Text]` reads `EDITOR`:
      a member name in camel case becomes the conventional upper-snake-case variable name
      (`xdgConfigHome` is `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`), and the type argument says what the value
      should decode to, through a `Variable` instance for that name and type. `Variable`'s
      companion has instances for the well-known variables (`home`, `path`, `shell`, the XDG
      ones) typed as paths, `columns` as an `Int`, and a generic fallback for any name as
      `Text` or as any type `Decodable in Text`. A name which is not a valid identifier, or
      which is only known at runtime, is read with `Environment[Text](t"NAME")`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val lang: Text = Environment.lang[Text]
      val width: Int = Environment.columns[Int]
      val custom: Text = Environment[Text](t"MY_SETTING")
      ```

      Both forms need an `Environment` in scope and raise `Environment.Error` when the variable
      is not defined; a `safely(…)` around the read turns a missing variable into `Unset`.

      The environment is a capability, so a block can run against a different one, and a given
      in a narrower scope wins:

      ```scala fuel
      alternative The JVM
        code
            locally:
              import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
              safely(Environment.editor[Text])   // whatever EDITOR is in the shell which started the JVM
      alternative Empty
        code
            locally:
              import environments.emptyEnvironment
              safely(Environment.editor[Text])   // Unset
      alternative For a test
        code
            locally:
              given Environment = name => if name == t"EDITOR" then t"vim" else Unset
              safely(Environment.editor[Text])   // t"vim"
      alternative Overlaid
        code
            locally:
              import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
              variables(editor = t"nano"):
                safely(Environment.editor[Text])   // t"nano"; every other variable as before
      ##
      ```

      A resident daemon serves invocations from many shells, each with its own environment,
      and the JVM's global one belongs to whichever shell started the daemon: in a daemon the
      invocation's environment is `environments.daemonClientEnvironment` (from `exoskeleton`),
      and the launching one `environments.daemonEnvironment` (from `ethereal`).

      An `Environment` is `Findable`, so a `Path` can be resolved relative to it where a
      filesystem library offers that.
  refer ambience.Variable
  refer ambience.variables
  refer ambience.environments
  refer ambience.System
  entry .apply   reads a variable by its runtime name, decoded to the type asked for
      `Environment[Text](t"HOME")` reads the variable named by the text, through the
      `Variable[Label, T]` instance for the type (`Text`, or any type `Decodable in Text`), and
      raises `Environment.Error` if it is not defined. It needs an `Environment` in scope. This
      is the form for a name which is not known until runtime or which is not an identifier;
      a well-known variable is more simply a member access, `Environment.home[Text]`.
  entry .selectDynamic   the member-access form: `Environment.xdgConfigHome[Text]` reads `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`
      Any identifier is a member of `Environment`: the camel-case name is translated to
      upper-snake-case (`xdgConfigHome` to `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, `term` to `TERM`) by the
      `Variable` instance's `defaultName`, the variable is read from the `Environment` in
      scope, and its text is decoded by the instance for the name and the type argument.
      `Environment.Error` is raised, through the `Tactic` in scope, when the variable is not
      defined. The type argument is usually needed: `Environment.home[Text]`, or
      `Environment.home[Path on Linux]` where the `Variable.home` instance reads a path of
      any type `Instantiable across Paths from Text`.
  entry .provided   an `Environment` from any `Environment.Provider` in scope
      A given in the companion, so it needs no import: wherever a `Provider` — such as a CLI
      invocation, which carries the environment it was called from — is a given, an
      `Environment` is summonable directly.
  entry .Provider   something which carries a canonical environment, such as a CLI invocation
    detail
        A trait with one member, `#environment`. A type which has an environment of its own
        (an invocation of a daemon, say) extends it, and `Environment.provided` makes that
        environment the one in scope wherever the provider is a given.
    entry #environment   the environment this provider carries
  entry .Error   raised when a variable which was read is not defined
    detail
        Error 869: _the environment variable `variable` was not defined_. Raised by
        `Environment.apply` and the member-access form through the `Tactic[Environment.Error]`
        in scope; `safely(Environment.editor[Text])` reads as `Unset` instead.
    entry #variable   the name of the variable which was not defined, as it was looked up (`EDITOR`)
  entry #variable   the value of a variable by name, or `Unset` if it is not defined
      The one abstract method: the raw text of the variable, undecoded. `Environment.apply`
      and the member-access form call this and decode the result.
  entry #entries   every variable, where the environment can enumerate them; `Unset` where it cannot
      The JVM's environment and the empty environment can list themselves; one built from a
      lambda cannot, and answers `Unset`. A child process started under an environment which
      can be enumerated is given exactly these variables, which is why `variables(…)` adds its
      overrides to the entries as well as to lookups.

entry .Variable   how one environment variable is named and decoded, by its camel-case label
  detail
      `Variable[alias, T]` is the typeclass behind `Environment.alias[T]`: for a label such as
      `"xdgConfigHome"` it knows the variable's real name (`#defaultName`, which is `#name` if
      the instance overrides it and otherwise the label in upper-snake-case) and how to
      `#read` its text as a `T`.

      The companion has instances for the well-known variables, each keyed by its label:
      `home`, `mail`, `shell`, `oldpwd`, `windowid`, `editor`, `pager`, `manpager`,
      `sshAuthSock`, `xdgDataHome`, `xdgConfigHome`, `xdgBinHome`, `xdgStateHome`,
      `xdgCacheHome` and `xdgRuntimeDir` read a single path of any type `Instantiable across
      Paths from Text`; `path`, `xdgDataDirs` and `xdgConfigDirs` read a list of paths, split
      on the `path.separator` property of the `System` in scope (or `:` where it has none);
      `columns` reads an `Int`; and `lang`, `display` and `term` read `Text`. From the
      `Protovariable` parent come the two fallbacks for any label: `generic` reads the text as
      it is, and `decoder` reads it as any type `Decodable in Text`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val home: Path on Linux = Environment.home[Path on Linux]
      val searchpath: List[Path on Linux] = Environment.path[List[Path on Linux]]
      summon[Variable["xdgDataHome", Text]].defaultName   // t"XDG_DATA_HOME"
      ```

      A program may declare its own instance for a variable, as a given `Variable["myVar",
      MyType]`, and `Environment.myVar[MyType]` reads it.
  refer ambience.Environment
  refer ambience.Protovariable
  entry .path   `PATH`, as a list of paths split on the platform's path separator
      Needs a `System` for the `path.separator` property, falling back to `:`.
  entry .xdgDataDirs   `XDG_DATA_DIRS`, as a list of paths
  entry .xdgConfigDirs   `XDG_CONFIG_DIRS`, as a list of paths
  entry .xdgDataHome   `XDG_DATA_HOME`, as a path
  entry .xdgConfigHome   `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, as a path
  entry .xdgBinHome   `XDG_BIN_HOME`, as a path
  entry .xdgStateHome   `XDG_STATE_HOME`, as a path
  entry .xdgCacheHome   `XDG_CACHE_HOME`, as a path
  entry .xdgRuntimeDir   `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, as a path
  entry .home   `HOME`, as a path
  entry .mail   `MAIL`, as a path
  entry .shell   `SHELL`, as a path
  entry .oldpwd   `OLDPWD`, as a path
  entry .windowid   `WINDOWID`, as a path
  entry .editor   `EDITOR`, as a path
  entry .pager   `PAGER`, as a path
  entry .manpager   `MANPAGER`, as a path
  entry .sshAuthSock   `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`, as a path
  entry .columns   `COLUMNS`, as an `Int`
  entry .lang   `LANG`, as text
  entry .display   `DISPLAY`, as text
  entry .term   `TERM`, as text
  entry #defaultName   the variable's real name: `#name` if set, otherwise the label in upper-snake-case
      `"xdgDataHome"` becomes `XDG_DATA_HOME` and `"term"` becomes `TERM`.
  entry #name   an explicit variable name, overriding the derived one; `Unset` by default
  entry #read   decodes the variable's text to the instance's type

entry .Protovariable   the fallback `Variable` instances for any label: as text, or as any decodable type
  detail
      The parent of the `Variable` companion, holding the two instances which apply to every
      label, at lower priority than the named ones in `Variable` itself: so
      `Environment.anything[Text]` reads any variable as text, and `Environment.anything[Int]`
      decodes it with the `Int is Decodable in Text` instance. Not normally referred to
      directly.
  entry #generic   any variable, as its raw text
  entry #decoder   any variable, decoded to a type which is `Decodable in Text`

entry .variables   runs a block with named variables laid over the environment in scope
  detail
      `variables(name = value, …)(block)` names each variable in camel case and sets it in the
      conventional upper-snake-case form — `gitIndexFile` is `GIT_INDEX_FILE` — on top of the
      `Environment` in scope, for the duration of the block, which takes the new environment as
      a context parameter. Lookups of other names fall through to the outer environment, and
      the overrides are added to `Environment#entries` too, so a subprocess started within the
      block inherits them.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      variables(gitIndexFile = t"/tmp/index", home = t"/home/juliet"):
        (Environment.gitIndexFile[Text], Environment.home[Text])   // (t"/tmp/index", t"/home/juliet")
      ```

      The values are `Text`; an outer `Environment` is required.
  entry .applyDynamicNamed   the named-argument application behind `variables(name = value)(block)`
      Not called by name: the compiler rewrites `variables(gitIndexFile = t"…")` to it. Each
      argument name is converted from camel case to upper snake case, and the block receives
      the extended `Environment` as a context parameter.

entry .System   the system properties, as a contextual value, and the platform read from them
  detail
      A `System` answers a property's name with its text, or `Unset`; the JVM's own is
      `systems.javaBaseSystem`, imported by name, and `systems.emptySystem` defines nothing.
      A lambda from name to `Optional[Text]` is a `System` for a test.

      Properties are read through `.properties`, with the dotted name written as a path of
      members ending in an application whose type argument says what the value decodes to:
      `System.properties.user.home[Text]()`, `System.properties.file.separator[Char]()`. The
      companion's `Property` instances know the types of the standard properties (`java.home`
      and `user.home` as paths, `os.arch` as an `Architecture`, `java.class.version` as an
      `Int`), and any other property reads as `Text` or as any type `Decodable in Text`. A
      property which is not defined raises `Property.Error`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem

      val user: Text = System.properties.user.name[Text]()
      val tmp: Text = System.properties.java.io.tmpdir[Text]()
      val os: Os = System.os
      val arch: Architecture = System.architecture
      ```

      Properties and environment variables are alike in being textual key/value data, and
      differ in where they come from: a property is passed as a `-D` argument to the JVM and
      named in dotted lower case, a variable is inherited from the shell and named in upper
      snake case. Each is decoded at the point of reading rather than passed inward as text.

      In a file which imports `soundness.*`, this `System` shadows `java.lang.System`: write
      `java.lang.System.nanoTime` for the Java one.
  refer ambience.Property
  refer ambience.systems
  refer ambience.Os
  refer ambience.Architecture
  entry .os   the running operating system, decoded from the `os.name` property
      `System.os` reads `os.name` from the `System` in scope and decodes it as an `Os`, so a
      program matches on `Os.MacOs` rather than on the many spellings the property takes. It
      panics, rather than raising, if the property is not defined, as the typed property reads
      do; a lenient check is `system(t"os.name").let(_.as[Os])`.
  entry .architecture   the running processor architecture, decoded from the `os.arch` property
      `System.architecture` reads `os.arch` and decodes it as an `Architecture`, `Arm(64)` for
      `aarch64` or `arm64`. Panics if the property is not defined.
  entry .properties   the root of the dotted-name path for reading a property
    detail
        `System.properties.a.b.c[T]()` reads the property `a.b.c`: each member access extends
        a `Property.Access` with one more segment, and the final application reads the
        property from the `System` in scope through the `Property` instance for that name and
        type. `System.properties[T](t"a.b.c")` reads a property by its runtime name.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import systems.javaBaseSystem

        System.properties.java.version[Text]()
        System.properties.path.separator[Char]()
        System.properties[Text](t"java.vendor")
        ```
    entry .apply   reads a property by its runtime name, decoded to the type asked for
        Needs a `System`, and a `Property` instance for the type: `Text`, or any type
        `Decodable in Text`. Raises `Property.Error` if the property is not defined.
    entry .selectDynamic   begins a dotted property name: `System.properties.user` is a `Property.Access["user"]`
  entry #apply   the value of a property by name, or `Unset` if it is not defined
      The one abstract method, the raw text. The typed reads call this and decode the result.

entry .Property   how one system property, by its dotted name, is decoded to a type
  detail
      `name is Property of T` is the typeclass behind `System.properties.<name>[T]()`: for the
      literal property name it knows how to `#read` the optional text as a `T`, and what to do
      when the property is missing. The companion has instances for the standard JVM
      properties, so each reads as its natural type: `java.home`, `user.home` and `user.dir`
      as a path of any type `Instantiable across Paths from Text`; `file.separator` and
      `path.separator` as a `Char`; `os.arch` as an `Architecture`; `java.class.version` as an
      `Int` (`65` for Java 21); and `java.version`, `java.vendor`, `java.vendor.url`,
      `java.runtime.version`, `line.separator`, `user.name`, `os.name` and `os.version` as
      `Text`. Any other property reads as `Text` (`.generic`) or as any type `Decodable in
      Text` (`.decoder`), each raising `Property.Error` through the `Tactic` in scope if it is
      not defined.

      The standard instances with a fixed type panic rather than raise on a missing property,
      since a JVM always defines them; the generic ones, and `javaRuntimeVersion` and
      `javaClassVersion`, need a `Tactic[Property.Error]`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem

      val home: Path on Linux = System.properties.user.home[Path on Linux]()
      val classVersion: Int = System.properties.java.`class`.version[Int]()
      val custom: Int = System.properties.myapp.port[Int]()
      ```

      A property name which is a Scala keyword, such as `class` in `java.class.path`, is
      written in backticks.
  refer ambience.System
  entry .apply   makes a `Property` instance from a function on the property's text
      `Property[name, T](text => …)` is the instance which decodes a defined property with the
      function and panics if it is undefined; it is how the companion's fixed-type instances
      are written, and how a program declares the type of a property of its own.
  entry .generic   any property, as its raw text; raises `Property.Error` if undefined
  entry .decoder   any property, decoded to a type which is `Decodable in Text`; raises `Property.Error` if undefined
  entry .javaHome   `java.home`, as a path of any type
  entry .javaVersion   `java.version`, as text
  entry .javaVendor   `java.vendor`, as text
  entry .javaVendorUrl   `java.vendor.url`, as text
  entry .javaRuntimeVersion   `java.runtime.version`, as text (`21.0.2+13-LTS`); raises `Property.Error` if undefined
  entry .javaClassVersion   `java.class.version`, as the major class-file version (`65` for Java 21)
      The property is a decimal such as `65.0`; its integer part is what is wanted. Raises
      `Property.Error` if the property is undefined or does not parse.
  entry .fileSeparator   `file.separator`, as a `Char`
  entry .pathSeparator   `path.separator`, as a `Char`
  entry .lineSeparator   `line.separator`, as text
  entry .userName   `user.name`, as text
  entry .userHome   `user.home`, as a path of any type
  entry .userDir   `user.dir`, as a path of any type
  entry .osName   `os.name`, as text; `System.os` decodes it as an `Os`
  entry .osVersion   `os.version`, as text
  entry .osArch   `os.arch`, as an `Architecture`
  entry .Access   a partly-written dotted property name, extended by member access and read by application
    detail
        `System.properties.user` is a `Property.Access["user"]`; `.home` extends it to
        `Property.Access["user.home"]`, and `[Text]()` reads it. The name is carried in the
        type, so the `Property` instance is chosen by the full dotted name at compile time.
    entry #selectDynamic   extends the name by one dotted segment
    entry #applyDynamic   extends the name by one segment and reads the property in a single step
    entry #property   the dotted property name written so far, as a string
    entry #apply   reads the named property from the `System` in scope, decoded to the type argument
  entry .Error   raised when a property which was read is not defined
    detail
        Error 815: _the system property `property` was not defined_. Raised by the `generic`
        and `decoder` instances, and by `javaRuntimeVersion` and `javaClassVersion`, through
        the `Tactic[Property.Error]` in scope; also by other libraries which read a property
        they need, such as `LocalClasspath` reading `path.separator`.
    entry #property   the dotted name of the property which was not defined
  entry #read   decodes a property's optional text, given its name, to the instance's type
  entry #Self   the property's name, as a literal string type

entry .Os   the operating system a program runs on, as a value decoded from `os.name`
  detail
      An enum of the operating systems the JVM reports — `Linux`, `MacOs`, `Windows`,
      `FreeBsd`, `OpenBsd`, `NetBsd`, `Solaris`, `Aix` — and `Other(name)` for one it does not
      know, keeping the name. `System.os` reads the running one from the `System` in scope,
      and `text.as[Os]` decodes any `os.name` text, matching on the lowercased prefix so that
      `Mac OS X`, `Windows Server 2022` and `SunOS` each decode as expected.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import systems.javaBaseSystem

      t"Mac OS X".as[Os]              // Os.MacOs
      t"Windows Server 2022".as[Os]   // Os.Windows
      t"Plan 9".as[Os]                // Os.Other(t"Plan 9")

      val asset: Optional[Text] = (System.os, System.architecture) match
        case (Os.Linux, Architecture.X86(64)) => t"linux-x64"
        case (Os.MacOs, Architecture.Arm(64)) => t"macos-arm64"
        case _                                => Unset
      ```

      `Os` is not the target of `Vm.intercept`; the virtual machine's events are intercepted
      through `Vm`.
  refer ambience.System
  refer ambience.Architecture
  entry .decodable   reads an `os.name` text as an `Os`, by its lowercased prefix
      `linux`; `mac` or `darwin`; `windows`; `freebsd`; `openbsd`; `netbsd`; `sunos` or
      `solaris`; `aix`; anything else is `Other(name)`. Decoding never fails.

entry .Architecture   the processor architecture, as a value decoded from `os.arch`
  detail
      An enum of processor families with their word size: `X86(32 | 64)`, `Arm(32 | 64)`,
      `Ppc(32 | 64, littleEndian)`, `Sparc(32 | 64)`, `Mips(32 | 64)`, `S390(31 | 64)`,
      `RiscV`, and `Other(name)` for one it does not know. `System.architecture` reads the
      running one from the `System` in scope, so code which chooses a native library or a code
      path by architecture matches on a value rather than on a string whose spelling varies by
      platform.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      t"amd64".as[Architecture]     // Architecture.X86(64)
      t"aarch64".as[Architecture]   // Architecture.Arm(64)
      t"ppc64le".as[Architecture]   // Architecture.Ppc(64, true)
      ```
  refer ambience.System
  refer ambience.Os
  entry .decodable   reads an `os.arch` text as an `Architecture`
      Decoding never fails; an unrecognised name is kept as `Other(name)`:

      ```tabular
      column  `os.arch`
      column  Architecture
      row  `x86`, `i386`  `X86(32)`
      row  `x86_64`, `amd64`  `X86(64)`
      row  `arm`  `Arm(32)`
      row  `aarch64`, `arm64`  `Arm(64)`
      row  `ppc`  `Ppc(32)`
      row  `ppc64`  `Ppc(64)`
      row  `ppc64le`  `Ppc(64, true)`, little-endian
      row  `sparc`  `Sparc(32)`
      row  `sparcv9`  `Sparc(64)`
      row  `mips`  `Mips(32)`
      row  `mips64`  `Mips(64)`
      row  `riscv64`  `RiscV`
      row  `s390`  `S390(31)`
      row  `s390x`  `S390(64)`
      row  any other name  `Other(name)`
      ```

entry .Vm   the virtual machine the program runs in: the target of `Vm.intercept`
    An object with no members of its own. It is where the events the virtual machine raises —
    its shutdown, an uncaught exception, a garbage collection — are intercepted, through
    `Vm.intercept[Shutdown]`, `Vm.intercept[Fault]` and `Vm.intercept[GarbageCollection]` in
    the `parasite` library, which declares those events as interceptable on this target. Only
    the JVM provides the events today, but none of them is JVM-specific in principle, which is
    why the target is `Vm` and not the staging rig `Jvm`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    Vm.intercept[Shutdown]:
      ()   // runs as the virtual machine shuts down
    ```

entry .WorkingDirectory   the directory a program was launched in, as a contextual value
  detail
      A `WorkingDirectory` names one directory, as `#directory()` text; a method which
      resolves a relative path against the working directory takes one as a `using`
      parameter, rather than reading `user.dir` from a global. The ones to import by name are
      in the `workingDirectories` package: `javaBaseWorkingDirectory` is the JVM's `user.dir`,
      `systemWorkingDirectory` reads `user.dir` from the `System` in scope,
      `wasiWorkingDirectory` a Wasm component's initial directory, and
      `daemonClientWorkingDirectory` (from `ethereal`) the directory each invocation of a
      daemon was made from. `WorkingDirectory(path)` makes one from any path value, and the
      top-level `workingDirectory[Path on Linux]` resolves the one in scope to a path type.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val cwd: Path on Linux = workingDirectory[Path on Linux]
      val fixed: WorkingDirectory = WorkingDirectory(t"/tmp/work")
      fixed.directory()   // t"/tmp/work"
      ```
  refer ambience.workingDirectories
  refer ambience.workingDirectory
  entry .apply   a working directory from any path type which is `Abstractable across Paths to Text`
  entry .provided   a `WorkingDirectory` from any `WorkingDirectory.Provider` in scope
      A given in the companion, so it needs no import: wherever a provider, such as a CLI
      invocation, is a given, its working directory is summonable directly.
  entry .Provider   something which carries a canonical working directory, such as a CLI invocation
    detail
        A trait with one member, `#workingDirectory`. Each client of a daemon has a working
        directory of its own, so the invocation type provides it, and `WorkingDirectory.provided`
        makes it the one in scope.
    entry #workingDirectory   the working directory this provider carries
  entry .Error   raised when a working directory is needed and there is none
    detail
        Error 913: _there is no working directory_. Raised by code which must resolve a
        relative path and has no `WorkingDirectory` to resolve it against, such as a daemon
        invocation whose client could not report one.
  entry #directory   the working directory, as text

entry .TemporaryDirectory   a directory for temporary files, as a contextual value
  detail
      A `TemporaryDirectory` names one directory as `#directory()` text, and `#path[T]`
      instantiates it as any path type. The ones to import by name are in the
      `temporaryDirectories` package: `javaBaseTemporaryDirectory` reads the JVM's
      `java.io.tmpdir`, `systemTemporaryDirectory` the same property from the `System` in
      scope, and `environmentTemporaryDirectory` the first of `TMPDIR`, `TMP` and `TEMP` from
      the `Environment` in scope. The top-level `temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux]` resolves
      the one in scope to a path type.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
      import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory

      val tmp: Path on Linux = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux]
      ```
  refer ambience.temporaryDirectories
  refer ambience.temporaryDirectory
  entry #directory   the temporary directory, as text
  entry #path   the temporary directory as a path of any type `Instantiable across Paths from Text`

entry .workingDirectory   the `WorkingDirectory` in scope, resolved to a path type: `workingDirectory[Path on Linux]`
    Needs a `WorkingDirectory` and a path type which is `Representative of Paths`. The text
    is instantiated through the type's trusted instantiation where it has one — a working
    directory comes from the operating system, so it is not validated — and otherwise from
    plain text. The method is `inline`, so it can be expanded inside a daemon's `cli` block.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
    import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

    workingDirectory[Path on Linux]
    ```

entry .temporaryDirectory   the `TemporaryDirectory` in scope, resolved to a path type: `temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux]`
    Needs a `TemporaryDirectory` and a path type which is `Representative of Paths`; the text
    is instantiated through the type's trusted instantiation, as `workingDirectory` does.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
    import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory

    temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux]
    ```

entry .Xdg   the XDG base directories, from the environment where set and the specification's defaults where not
  detail
      The XDG base directory specification says where a program's data, configuration, cache
      and state belong, honouring the user's environment (`XDG_DATA_HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_DIRS`,
      …) where it is set and falling back to the specification's defaults beneath the home
      directory (from the `user.home` property) where it is not. Each method takes the path
      type to return as a type argument, and needs an `Environment` and a `System` in scope.
      `Directories` is the same set of directories with the Windows locations substituted on
      Windows.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
      import systems.javaBaseSystem

      Xdg.configHome[Path on Linux]   // $XDG_CONFIG_HOME, or ~/.config
      Xdg.cacheHome[Path on Linux]    // $XDG_CACHE_HOME, or ~/.cache
      Xdg.dataDirs[Path on Linux]     // $XDG_DATA_DIRS, or /usr/local/share and /usr/share
      ```

      Using these rather than a hard-coded `~/.myapp` is what makes a program's files land
      where the user's backup, sync and cleanup tools expect them.

      `Xdg.Data` and `Xdg.Config` are the two XDG searchpath planes: a `Path on Xdg.Data`
      denotes a subpath searched across `dataHome` and every entry of `dataDirs`, in that
      order of precedence, and `Path on Xdg.Config` likewise across `configHome` and
      `configDirs`. The searching itself is in the `galilei` library.
  refer ambience.Directories
  refer ambience.Searchpath
  refer ambience.Environment
  entry .Data   the searchpath plane over `dataHome` and `dataDirs`, a `Searchpath`
      A `Path on Xdg.Data` is a subpath such as `myapp/schema.json`, resolved by searching the
      data home and then each data directory. The resolution lives in `galilei`.
  entry .Config   the searchpath plane over `configHome` and `configDirs`, a `Searchpath`
  entry .dataHome   `$XDG_DATA_HOME`, or `~/.local/share`
  entry .configHome   `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, or `~/.config`
  entry .cacheHome   `$XDG_CACHE_HOME`, or `~/.cache`
  entry .stateHome   `$XDG_STATE_HOME`, or `~/.local/state`
  entry .runtimeDir   `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, or `Unset`: the specification gives it no default
  entry .bin   `$XDG_BIN_HOME`, or `~/.local/bin`
  entry .dataDirs   `$XDG_DATA_DIRS` split on the path separator, or `/usr/local/share` and `/usr/share`
  entry .configDirs   `$XDG_CONFIG_DIRS` split on the path separator, or `/etc/xdg`

entry .Directories   the standard user directories, with the Windows locations on Windows and XDG's elsewhere
  detail
      The same directories as `Xdg`, chosen by platform: where the `System` in scope reports
      an `os.name` which decodes as `Os.Windows`, the data, config, cache and state homes are
      the roaming or local `AppData` directories (from `APPDATA` and `LOCALAPPDATA`, with the
      conventional defaults under the home directory where they are unset), the runtime
      directory and `bin` sit under local `AppData`, and the data and config directories are
      `PROGRAMDATA`; on every other platform, or where `os.name` is absent, each is the `Xdg`
      directory of the same name. Each method takes the path type as a type argument and
      needs an `Environment` and a `System`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import pathInterfaces.pathOnLinux
      import systems.javaBaseSystem

      Directories.configHome[Path on Linux]
      Directories.homeText   // the user.home property
      ```
  refer ambience.Xdg
  entry .home   the user's home directory, from `user.home`, as a path of any type
      Needs only a `System`; panics if `user.home` is not set, as a JVM always sets it.
  entry .homeText   the user's home directory, from `user.home`, as text
  entry .dataHome   roaming `AppData` on Windows; `Xdg.dataHome` elsewhere
  entry .configHome   roaming `AppData` on Windows; `Xdg.configHome` elsewhere
  entry .cacheHome   local `AppData` on Windows; `Xdg.cacheHome` elsewhere
  entry .stateHome   local `AppData` on Windows; `Xdg.stateHome` elsewhere
  entry .runtimeDir   `Temp` under local `AppData` on Windows; `Xdg.runtimeDir` elsewhere, which may be `Unset`
  entry .bin   `Programs` under local `AppData` on Windows; `Xdg.bin` elsewhere
  entry .dataDirs   `PROGRAMDATA` (or `C:\ProgramData`) on Windows; `Xdg.dataDirs` elsewhere
  entry .configDirs   `PROGRAMDATA` (or `C:\ProgramData`) on Windows; `Xdg.configDirs` elsewhere

entry .Searchpath   the marker for a path plane which searches an ordered list of real directories
  detail
      A _searchpath plane_ joins an ordered list of directories into a single searchable view
      — the XDG data directories, or `$PATH` — so that a `Path on Xdg.Data` names a subpath
      found in whichever directory has it first. A plane extends `Searchpath` to opt in, as
      `Xdg.Data` and `Xdg.Config` do; the companion then supplies the `serpentine` givens every
      such plane needs, in implicit scope: paths are rooted at `%`, the root encodes as
      nothing (so a path reads as `myapp/config.json`), and each segment is held to `.Rules`.

      Deliberately, a searchpath plane has no `Filesystem` instance: the absolute-path
      operations of `galilei`, bounded by a `Filesystem`, must not apply to a virtual path,
      which they would resolve as a literal platform path. The joining machinery — the ordered
      stems a plane resolves onto, first-match resolution and merged, shadowed listings — is
      `galilei`'s `Searchpaths`.
  refer ambience.Xdg
  entry .Rules   what a segment of a searchpath path may be
      A segment must not contain `/`, and must not be `.`, `..` or empty.
  entry .nominative   the given which holds searchpath paths to `.Rules`
  entry .submissible   the given which lets a path on any searchpath plane be built from the root `%`
  entry .radical   how the root `%` of a searchpath plane is written: as nothing
      The root is implicit in every entry's text, so a path on the plane encodes as its
      segments alone.
  entry .eradicable   how the root `%` of a searchpath plane is read: always `%`, consuming no text
      Reading the root cannot fail, so this given is pure and takes no tactic.

entry .Configurator   a source of an application's settings, keyed by camel-case name, composed by priority
  detail
      A `Configurator` reads a setting by its canonical camel-case name (`logLevel`) from one
      source, which owns the mapping into its native namespace: `Configurator.environment`
      reads `MYAPP_LOG_LEVEL` from the `Environment` in scope and `Configurator.properties`
      reads `myapp.log.level` from the `System`, each under the application's
      `Configurator.Prefix`. Configurators compose with `#++`, and composition order is
      priority order: the left source is consulted first, and a name it does not have falls
      through to the right.

      Declaring a `Prefix` given is what activates the standard cascade, `.default`: system
      properties take priority over environment variables, and the command line, which beats
      both, is handled by whichever type consults the configurator. A `Configurator` given in
      lexical scope overrides the default, so a configuration file in any format joins the
      cascade as a lambda from name to `Optional[Text]`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import systems.javaBaseSystem

      given Configurator.Prefix = Configurator.Prefix(t"myapp")
      val file: Configurator = name => if name == t"port" then t"7070" else Unset

      (Configurator.properties ++ Configurator.environment ++ file).read(t"port")
      ```

      A configurator is a capability: it reaches outside the program, so the type is tracked
      wherever it appears, and a method which reads settings takes `using Configurator`. It is
      shared, not exclusive, since reading is free of side effects.
  refer ambience.Environment
  refer ambience.System
  entry .Prefix   the application's name, which prefixes its variables (`MYAPP_`) and properties (`myapp.`)
    detail
        Declared once as a given, `given Configurator.Prefix = Configurator.Prefix(t"myapp")`:
        it is upper-cased for the environment and lower-cased for properties.
    entry #name   the application's name
  entry .environment   the configurator over the `Environment` in scope: `logLevel` reads `MYAPP_LOG_LEVEL`
      The camel-case name is converted to upper snake case and prefixed with the upper-cased
      `Prefix` and an underscore. Needs a `Prefix` and an `Environment`.
  entry .properties   the configurator over the `System` in scope: `logLevel` reads `myapp.log.level`
      The camel-case name is converted to dotted lower case and prefixed with the lower-cased
      `Prefix` and a dot. Needs a `Prefix` and a `System`.
  entry .default   the standard cascade, properties before environment, activated by a `Prefix` given
      `properties ++ environment`. A given in the companion, so it is summoned wherever a
      `Prefix`, an `Environment` and a `System` are in scope; a `Configurator` given in lexical
      scope outranks it.
  entry #read   the setting's text by its camel-case name, or `Unset` if this source does not have it
  entry #++   composes two configurators, this one taking priority
      `this ++ that` reads from `this` and falls through to `that` for a name it does not have.
      The result is a fresh configurator capturing both.

entry .WasiEnvironmentApi   the WIT interface type of `wasi:cli/environment`, as `ambience.wasi` binds it
    `Interface in Wit at "/ambience/environment.wit"`: the type of the `wasiApis.wasiEnvironmentApi`
    given, against which `environments.wasiEnvironment` and
    `workingDirectories.wasiWorkingDirectory` are typechecked. Only in the `ambience.wasi`
    source set.

entry .environments   the environments to import by name: the JVM's, the empty one, a daemon's, a Wasm component's
  detail
      Which environment `Environment.editor[Text]` reads from is the `Environment` given in
      scope, and the choice is never implicit: `import soundness.*` brings none of these in,
      and one is imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      ```

      `javaBaseEnvironment` suits an ordinary program. `emptyEnvironment` is for tests and
      sandboxes. The two daemon environments, shipped by other libraries, distinguish the
      invocation's environment from the one the daemon was launched with. An `Environment` is
      a single function from name to `Optional[Text]`, so a custom one is a lambda bound as a
      local given.
  entry .javaBaseEnvironment   the environment the JVM was started with, from `java.lang.System.getenv`
      Enumerable: `entries` is the whole environment. In a daemon this is not any invocation's
      environment: the launcher starts the daemon with a sanitized environment, and each
      invocation's own arrives with it as `daemonClientEnvironment`.
  entry .emptyEnvironment   an environment which defines nothing; `entries` is the empty map
      For a test or a sandbox: every read is `Unset`, so every typed read raises
      `Environment.Error`.
  entry .wasiEnvironment   a Wasm component's environment, from `wasi:cli/environment`'s `get-environment`
      In the `ambience.wasi` source set. An `inline` given, so that the Wasm Component import
      materialises at the site which summons it, in code compiled for a Wasm target; a
      WASI-linked application summons it once. It cannot enumerate: `entries` is `Unset`.
  entry .daemonEnvironment   the environment of the invocation which started the daemon; shipped by `ethereal`
      The launcher starts a daemon with a sanitized environment of its own, so that the
      daemon's state does not depend on who happened to start it, and sends the environment
      it was itself started with on the daemon's standard input. This given is that
      environment, held in memory. Few things want it rather than the current invocation's
      environment, which is `daemonClientEnvironment`; without a launcher (a JVM run
      directly) it is the JVM's own environment.
  entry .daemonClientEnvironment   the environment of the current invocation of a daemon; shipped by `exoskeleton`
      Needs a `Cli` in scope, the invocation, and is its environment: the variables of the
      shell which ran the command, not of the shell which started the daemon. This is the
      environment a daemon's command handler should read.

entry .systems   the system-property sources to import by name: the JVM's, or an empty one
  detail
      The `System` in scope is where `System.properties.user.home[Text]()`, `System.os` and
      the standard directories read from. `import soundness.*` does not bring one in; one is
      imported by name, or a lambda from name to `Optional[Text]` is bound as a local given
      for a test.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      ```
  entry .javaBaseSystem   the JVM's properties, from `java.lang.System.getProperty`
  entry .emptySystem   a system which defines no properties
      For a test which must not depend on the JVM it runs under: every read is `Unset`, so
      every typed read raises `Property.Error` or, for the fixed-type standard properties,
      panics.

entry .workingDirectories   the working directories to import by name: the JVM's, the `System`'s, a daemon client's, a Wasm component's
  detail
      The `WorkingDirectory` in scope is what `workingDirectory[Path on Linux]` resolves, and
      what a relative path is resolved against. One is imported by name:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      ```

      In a daemon, the directory each invocation was made from is
      `daemonClientWorkingDirectory`, not the JVM's.
  entry .javaBaseWorkingDirectory   the JVM's `user.dir` property
      `systemWorkingDirectory` specialised to `systems.javaBaseSystem`.
  entry .systemWorkingDirectory   `user.dir` read from the `System` in scope
      Needs a `System`, which lets a test fix the working directory by fixing the system;
      panics if the property is not set.
  entry .wasiWorkingDirectory   a Wasm component's initial directory, from `wasi:cli/environment`'s `initial-cwd`
      In the `ambience.wasi` source set; an `inline` given, materialised where it is summoned.
      A component with no initial directory falls back to `/`.
  entry .daemonClientWorkingDirectory   the directory the current daemon invocation was made from; shipped by `ethereal`
      Needs a `Cli` in scope, the invocation, and is its working directory. Each client of a
      daemon has its own, which the JVM's `user.dir` — the directory the daemon was started
      in — says nothing about.

entry .temporaryDirectories   the temporary directories to import by name: by property, by `System`, or by environment
  detail
      The `TemporaryDirectory` in scope is what `temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux]` resolves.
      One is imported by name:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory
      ```
  entry .javaBaseTemporaryDirectory   the JVM's `java.io.tmpdir` property
      Panics if the property is not set, which a JVM always sets.
  entry .systemTemporaryDirectory   `java.io.tmpdir`, requiring a `System` in scope
      Takes a `System` so that the dependence is declared, though the value read is the
      JVM's own `java.io.tmpdir` property.
  entry .environmentTemporaryDirectory   the first of `TMPDIR`, `TMP` and `TEMP` in the `Environment` in scope
      Needs an `Environment`; panics if none of the three variables is set.

entry .termcaps   the terminal capabilities read from the environment, as a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Termcap` says what the terminal can do — whether it takes ANSI escapes, its colour
      depth, its width and height — and styled output is rendered to fit it. The fixed ones
      (`basicTermcap`, `xterm256Termcap`, `xtermTrueColorTermcap`) are in the
      `anticipation` library's `termcapDefinitions` package; this package's
      `environmentTermcap` works the capabilities out from the environment.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import termcaps.environmentTermcap
      ```
  entry .environmentTermcap   a terminal described by `COLUMNS` and `COLORTERM`, or by `tput colors`
      Needs an `Environment`. ANSI escapes are assumed. The width is `COLUMNS` where the
      invoking context set it; where it is absent or malformed — output redirected to a file
      or a pipe, say — the width is unbounded and nothing wraps. The colour depth is
      `TrueColor` where `COLORTERM` is `truecolor`, and otherwise is read by running `tput
      colors`, falling back to `NoColor` if that fails. Both are computed once, lazily, when
      first asked for.

entry .wasiApis   the WIT interface definitions of `ambience.wasi`, as givens to import by name
  detail
      The `call` materialiser of a WASI import consults the interface in scope for the
      function's module id. Only in the `ambience.wasi` source set.
  entry .wasiEnvironmentApi   the `wasi:cli/environment` interface, from the bundled `/ambience/environment.wit`
      A `WasiEnvironmentApi`, read from the classpath resource. Import it by name where
      `environments.wasiEnvironment` or `workingDirectories.wasiWorkingDirectory` is used.
